Q: Passing a class type as a variable in Dart A: <p>You can put the &quot;YourClass.new&quot; unnamed constructor in a Dart variable :</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>class Dodo
{
  void hello() {
    print(&quot;hello dodo&quot;);
  }
}

void main() {

var a = Dodo.new;
var b = a();
b.hello();

}
</code></pre>

Q: case select only uses first two cases A: <p>This <code>Select .. Case</code> construction can be greatly simplified, and made suitable not only for integers:</p>
<pre class="lang-vb prettyprint-override"><code>Select Case F
    Case &lt;     50 : Celebrity_Lbl.Text = &quot;Elon&quot;
    Case &lt;    500 : Celebrity_Lbl.Text = &quot;Clay&quot;
    Case &lt;  10000 : Celebrity_Lbl.Text = &quot;Doug Bowser&quot;
    Case &lt; 500000 : Celebrity_Lbl.Text = &quot;Mathew Patrick&quot;
    Case Else     : Celebrity_Lbl.Text = &quot;Mark Edward Fischbach&quot;
End Select
</code></pre>

Q: How can I create a database for a MongoDB container on startup? A: <p>An add-on to a couple of answers here:</p>
<p>I would add a bit of code if the user you want to create needs to be attached to a specific database</p>
<p>The main thing is that you need to be logged on your administrator base to create new users.</p>
<p>So for the *docker-compose.yml examples, your <em>mongo-init.js</em> file would look like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>db = db.getSiblingDB('admin');
// Move to the administrator database. It is always created in MongoDB

db.auth(&quot;rootUser&quot;, &quot;rootPassword&quot;);
// Log as root administrator if you decided to authenticate in your Docker Compose file...

db = db.getSiblingDB('DB_test');
// Create and move to your new database

db.createUser({
    'user' : &quot;dbUser&quot;,
    'pwd'  : &quot;dbPwd&quot;,
    'roles': [{
        'role': 'dbOwner',
        'db'  : 'DB_test'}]});
// User created

db.createCollection('collection_test');
// Add the new collection
</code></pre>
